1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:10,649
You can put a top-end graphics card into a PC and get only a little over half the frame rate it's capable of, because the processor feeding it can't keep up.

2
00:00:10,929 --> 00:00:22,138
By the end of this video you'll know how to tell, in about a minute, whether your CPU or your GPU is the part setting your frame rate, and what to do about it without spending anything.

3
00:00:22,888 --> 00:00:25,799
A bottleneck is just the narrowest part of a pipe.

4
00:00:26,079 --> 00:00:32,775
Water can only flow as fast as the tightest section allows, no matter how wide the rest of the pipe is.

5
00:00:33,055 --> 00:00:34,651
A game works the same way.

6
00:00:34,931 --> 00:00:37,388
For every frame, the processor goes first.

7
00:00:37,668 --> 00:00:44,803
It runs the game logic, moves the characters, works out the physics, and writes a list of drawing instructions.

8
00:00:45,083 --> 00:00:48,622
The graphics card takes that list and turns it into pixels.

9
00:00:48,902 --> 00:00:52,898
Whichever of the two takes longer per frame sets your frame rate.

10
00:00:53,178 --> 00:00:54,776
The other one simply waits.

11
00:00:55,526 --> 00:00:58,988
That means every PC has a bottleneck, all the time.

12
00:00:59,268 --> 00:01:02,567
It isn't a fault, and it isn't something you can get rid of.

13
00:01:02,847 --> 00:01:13,273
If your graphics card is the slower stage, that's actually the ideal case, because you're getting everything you paid for out of the most expensive part in the box.

14
00:01:13,553 --> 00:01:17,172
So the useful question isn't whether you have a bottleneck.

15
00:01:17,452 --> 00:01:19,802
It's which part it is, and by how much.

16
00:01:20,552 --> 00:01:24,840
To see this clearly, let's follow one real test from start to finish.

17
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:43,091
The hardware site TechSpot took a budget Intel Core i3 13100, a four core chip, and paired it with three graphics cards in turn: an RTX 3060, an RTX 3080, and an RTX 4090.

18
00:01:43,371 --> 00:01:49,919
Then they ran the same tests with a Core i9 13900K for comparison.

19
00:01:50,199 --> 00:01:53,609
The game was Watch Dogs Legion at very high settings.

20
00:01:53,889 --> 00:01:55,527
Same game, same settings.

21
00:01:55,807 --> 00:01:57,266
Only the parts change.

22
00:01:58,016 --> 00:02:02,485
Start with the RTX 3060 at 1080p.

23
00:02:02,765 --> 00:02:09,502
With this card, the expensive Core i9 is just 3 percent faster than the little Core i3.

24
00:02:09,782 --> 00:02:10,765
Three percent.

25
00:02:11,045 --> 00:02:17,548
The graphics card is the narrow section of the pipe, so it doesn't matter how wide the processor side is.

26
00:02:17,828 --> 00:02:22,352
Both CPUs finish their share of every frame early, and then they wait.

27
00:02:22,632 --> 00:02:28,106
If you owned the Core i3 and this card, buying a new processor would be money wasted.

28
00:02:28,856 --> 00:02:33,242
Now swap in the RTX 3080, a much faster card.

29
00:02:33,522 --> 00:02:36,782
Suddenly the Core i9 is 50 percent faster.

30
00:02:37,062 --> 00:02:39,427
Nothing about either processor changed.

31
00:02:39,707 --> 00:02:45,212
The graphics card simply got wide enough that the Core i3 became the narrowest point.

32
00:02:45,492 --> 00:02:47,814
Same CPU, different bottleneck.

33
00:02:48,094 --> 00:02:53,457
This is why a question like "is my processor good enough" has no answer on its own.

34
00:02:53,737 --> 00:02:58,203
It always depends on what it's paired with, and on what you're asking it to run.

35
00:02:58,953 --> 00:03:02,848
With the RTX 4090, the gap gets extreme.

36
00:03:03,128 --> 00:03:06,941
The Core i3 stops at 102 frames per second.

37
00:03:07,221 --> 00:03:12,357
The Core i9 reaches 186, which is 82 percent faster.

38
00:03:12,637 --> 00:03:15,008
And here's the part that surprises people.

39
00:03:15,288 --> 00:03:24,884
Even at 4K, the i3 still holds the 4090 back to 99 frames per second, while the i9 gets 149.

40
00:03:25,164 --> 00:03:36,129
Going from 1080p to 4K means four times as many pixels, and the Core i3's result barely moved, from 102 to 99.

41
00:03:36,409 --> 00:03:39,782
That flat line is the fingerprint of a CPU limit.

42
00:03:40,532 --> 00:03:43,186
Why doesn't resolution matter to the processor?

43
00:03:43,466 --> 00:03:47,463
Because its job for each frame is the same at any resolution.

44
00:03:47,743 --> 00:03:54,730
The same characters walk around, the same physics runs, and the same list of drawing instructions gets written.

45
00:03:55,010 --> 00:03:58,453
Only the graphics card's job grows with more pixels.

46
00:03:58,733 --> 00:04:06,493
So if raising the resolution barely changes your frame rate, the graphics card was waiting and the processor was the wall.

47
00:04:06,773 --> 00:04:15,830
What does load the processor is the world itself: crowds of characters, moving vehicles, physics, long draw distances, and ray tracing.

48
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:18,520
Here's how to check your own PC.

49
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:27,906
Turn on a performance overlay, like the one in MSI Afterburner or your graphics driver, and watch GPU usage while you play.

50
00:04:28,186 --> 00:04:33,824
If it stays close to 100 percent, your graphics card is the limit, and that's fine.

51
00:04:34,104 --> 00:04:41,564
If it sits well below that, and no frame cap or V-Sync is holding it back, your processor is the likely limit.

52
00:04:41,844 --> 00:04:45,228
Don't be fooled when total CPU usage looks low.

53
00:04:45,508 --> 00:04:52,451
Many games lean on just a few cores, so a few cores can be flat out while the overall number looks relaxed.

54
00:04:52,731 --> 00:05:03,622
That's why a six core Ryzen 5600X performs about the same as a sixteen core 5950X in most games.

55
00:05:04,372 --> 00:05:11,739
You may have seen bottleneck calculator websites that give a single percentage for any CPU and GPU pair.

56
00:05:12,019 --> 00:05:12,858
Skip them.

57
00:05:13,138 --> 00:05:20,471
Nobody has tested every combination, and the real number changes with every game, resolution and setting.

58
00:05:20,751 --> 00:05:26,825
You just watched the same Core i3 go from a 3 percent gap to an 82 percent gap.

59
00:05:27,105 --> 00:05:31,106
Real benchmarks of the games you actually play tell you far more.

60
00:05:31,386 --> 00:05:38,563
It's also why reviewers test processors at 1080p with the fastest graphics card they can get.

61
00:05:38,843 --> 00:05:48,109
It takes the GPU out of the way, so you see the processor's real ceiling, which is the number you'll run into after your next graphics upgrade.

62
00:05:48,859 --> 00:05:50,298
So what do you do about it?

63
00:05:50,578 --> 00:05:57,503
If you're GPU limited, lower the heaviest settings or turn on upscaling, which renders fewer pixels.

64
00:05:57,783 --> 00:06:00,775
If you're CPU limited, you get a small gift.

65
00:06:01,055 --> 00:06:09,802
Your graphics card has spare room, so you can raise resolution, textures or lighting quality and your frame rate will barely drop.

66
00:06:10,082 --> 00:06:15,567
Then turn down the settings that load the processor, like crowd density and view distance.

67
00:06:15,847 --> 00:06:22,631
If your frame rate is already above your monitor's refresh rate, a frame cap smooths things out and saves power.

68
00:06:22,911 --> 00:06:29,410
Only when the processor holds you well below the frame rate you want is a CPU upgrade worth the money.

69
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:31,553
So remember the pipe.

70
00:06:31,833 --> 00:06:38,993
The narrowest part sets the flow, and the cheapest fix always starts with finding that part before you buy anything.

71
00:06:39,273 --> 00:06:45,425
Next time, we're opening up the part that feeds every other component in the case: the power supply.

72
00:06:45,705 --> 00:06:50,654
You'll learn what watts and 80 Plus ratings really mean, and why cheap ones fail.

73
00:06:50,934 --> 00:06:52,615
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

